WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.730
- In this video, I'm going to teach you

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:04.890
a key consulting methodology
that is being used

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:07.810
by almost all management consulting firms,

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:11.410
including MBB consulting
firms like BCG or Bain.

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:15.210
But the best thing is you do
not need to work in consulting

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.030
to make use of this approach.

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:20.410
It is helpful in whatever
context you operate in

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:24.700
if you are position to solve
problems for your employer.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:26.960
In today's video, I'm
talking about the theory

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:30.420
and practice of hypothesis-driven
problem solving,

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.370
and trust me, guys,
this is really crucial.

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:36.200
It is one of the most important concepts.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.470
So sit tight, listen until the end,

00:00:38.470 --> 00:00:40.800
because in the end, I'm
going to give you some nice,

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.613
practical tips as well,
and let's do this together.

00:00:43.613 --> 00:00:45.470
Welcome to another coffee break

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:47.340
here on this channel with me.

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:50.080
My name is Heinrich, and on
my channel, Firm Learning,

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.590
I want to help you to become successful

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.780
in the first years of your career.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:55.613
As you know,

00:00:55.613 --> 00:00:57.830
I started my own consulting
career at Accenture.

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.810
I worked for the company
during my university studies,

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:03.410
and then, after university,
I joined McKinsey

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:05.630
and spent six years with the firm,

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:07.530
in the end, serving as a project lead.

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:09.550
So, let's start by better understanding

00:01:09.550 --> 00:01:12.320
what hypothesis-based
consulting actually is,

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.930
and I have a couple of
slides prepared for you guys,

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:16.150
so let's jump into it.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:19.210
So, what is hypothesis-based
problem solving?

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:22.360
So, it is an approach to
divide a project into tasks.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.850
It is way of thinking,

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.580
an approach to define the
project scope and outcome,

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:30.040
a prerequisite to focus data
collection and analysis,

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:33.650
an approach to solve unstructured
and complex problems.

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:35.650
So, I hope this already
gives you the impression,

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:36.483
helps you to understand

00:01:36.483 --> 00:01:38.830
that hypothesis-based problem solving

00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:42.530
is really at the core of
most consulting projects,

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.700
but let's get a little bit more tangible.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.420
And to me, it is important to stress

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.810
that this is not only a process

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:50.960
that is strictly limited to consulting,

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:53.120
but you can pretty much use and apply it

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.960
to every other business
problem that you have.

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:57.000
So, what do we see here?

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.040
And indeed, it always starts
with the business problem.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:00.970
There's a challenge

00:02:00.970 --> 00:02:03.510
that management of your company is facing.

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.380
Maybe profits are down,
maybe costs are exploding

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.140
in a specific business area.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:10.110
And here now, often this is a situation

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:12.360
where companies then engage a consultancy

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:14.250
to help them figure out the solution,

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:17.530
or they also my task and
internal team to look into it

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.010
and to come up with solutions.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:22.810
And now, to move from the
problem to the solution,

00:02:22.810 --> 00:02:24.310
you will need to have facts.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:25.850
You will need to conduct analysis.

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:29.250
Now, of course, in this way,
from problem to solution,

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:31.040
you often already have some facts.

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:33.860
There are some things
that you already know,

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.570
but of course, there are gaps.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:38.270
There are things that you do not yet know,

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:41.410
and that will be required
to really verify a solution

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.360
or just come up with
it in the first place.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:45.140
And now, what many young graduates,

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.450
also more unexperienced
business professionals,

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:49.147
will do is where they will say,

00:02:49.147 --> 00:02:51.580
"Until I have closed these gaps,

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.830
until I've really looked at everything,

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:56.970
until I really did my thorough research,

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.460
no way I can come up with a solution

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.030
until I did all of this, right?"

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:03.490
And this, my friends, is not the way

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:05.900
how you should operate
in the business world.

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:08.280
This is more of an academic approach.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:11.680
You need to look into everything,
you do all your research,

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.410
and then, maybe in the very end,

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:14.950
you can come up with a solution.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:16.820
But, and you will already know this,

00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:19.700
if you have watched my video
on top down communication,

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:21.670
that I will link now
here, somewhere above,

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:24.050
the key to being successful in business

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.780
is always to already
having some kind of goal,

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:30.350
some key message, some key
result already in your head.

00:03:30.350 --> 00:03:32.790
It is important to very
early on in the process,

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:35.820
to have an hypothesis-based
solution in your mind

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:39.550
for the problem that you are
now testing and analyzing for,

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:41.520
instead of now starting with nothing

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:42.910
and needing to look at everything

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:44.510
before you can come up with a solution.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:46.950
So, indeed, what you
will do when you follow

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:50.240
this hypothesis-driven problem
solving approach is that,

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.910
from day one, you close
the gaps with hypothesis.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.240
So, when you're faced
with a business problem,

00:03:56.240 --> 00:03:58.480
now, before starting
to look into anything,

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.480
before starting to conduct
any analysis at all,

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:03.610
ask yourself, "What do I already know?"

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:06.950
And now, what are the likely
hypothesis that you can make,

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:07.830
and based on that,

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:11.140
what is your day one
answer to the project?

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:14.370
Once you've established such
an hypothesized solution,

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:16.740
then based on that, you can now test it,

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:19.650
you can now refine it
into an iterative way,

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:21.290
come to a better solution.

00:04:21.290 --> 00:04:23.150
So, let's look into a tangible example.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:26.190
Let's imagine there is a
mobile payment service provider

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:28.250
who wants to enter the Brazilian market,

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:29.880
and as a consultant, on day one,

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:31.530
maybe you sit together with your team.

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:33.650
Maybe there is a more
senior guy in the room,

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:35.710
a partner, or some
experts from the clients.

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.547
You just sit together
and you ask yourself,

00:04:37.547 --> 00:04:39.660
"Okay, what is my hypothesis?"

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.270
What the answer should be?

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:44.350
In which country the market
entry is recommended?

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:47.270
Now, based on this very
first session, on day one,

00:04:47.270 --> 00:04:49.182
you hypothesize four key factors.

00:04:49.182 --> 00:04:50.700
First, the growth,

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:53.710
so you want to be in the
market with the highest growth.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.006
Second, the affinity to mobile payment,

00:04:56.006 --> 00:04:57.930
so which markets are more affined

00:04:57.930 --> 00:04:59.320
to mobile payments than others?

00:04:59.320 --> 00:05:01.590
Third, the attractiveness of the offering,

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:04.200
so for which countries do you
have an attractive offering?

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.360
And then, fourth, where is competition?

00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:08.120
Where is small and little competition?

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:10.560
So, these are the factors
that you believe are relevant,

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:11.980
and now, from your hypothesis,

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:14.310
from your first knowledge
that you already have,

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:17.100
you believe that Brazil
indeed might be a good fit

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:18.470
due to these factors.

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:20.770
But now, of course,
this is only the start,

00:05:20.770 --> 00:05:22.980
and now, indeed, you
need to collect the data.

00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:25.840
You need to look into it
and understand and verify

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:27.850
whether this is actually the case or not.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.770
So, now you do conduct the research,

00:05:29.770 --> 00:05:31.920
and now you indeed find that Brazil,

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:33.830
at least in terms of the market size,

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:36.040
maybe not the growth,
but in terms of the size,

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:36.873
it is very attractive,

00:05:36.873 --> 00:05:39.400
because it has a population
of 200 million people,

00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:40.810
and all these numbers are just made up.

00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:43.300
And on top of that,
Brazilians are also very open

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:45.130
and interested in mobile payment,

00:05:45.130 --> 00:05:46.960
making it very interesting as well,

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:48.510
because the affinity is quite high.

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:50.990
Maybe you were also able
to confirm that actually

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:54.330
our product offering is
attractive to Brazilian customers.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:56.060
Though, in the end, you
came to the conclusion

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:59.080
that, no, in Brazil, there is
actually a lot of competition.

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:00.470
There isn't little competition,

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:02.220
so you see that now you need to cut out

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:03.820
the point of little competition,

00:06:03.820 --> 00:06:07.290
because you were not able
to verify the hypothesis,

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:09.150
but still, based on these three factors,

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:11.130
now you were able to confirm.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.810
You still feel confident in
making the recommendation

00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:15.860
that the mobile payment service provider

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:18.710
should indeed enter the Brazilian market

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:20.160
for these three reasons.

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:22.350
Now, I hope that you are
already can understand

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:25.800
why this hypothesis-driven
approach is very helpful,

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:27.587
because on day one, you
are asking yourself,

00:06:27.587 --> 00:06:29.690
"Okay, what would it mean for a country

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:32.023
to be an attractive
target for market entry?"

00:06:32.023 --> 00:06:35.050
This now enables you to
give a day one answer,

00:06:35.050 --> 00:06:37.620
so back to the top-down
communication approach,

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:40.870
if now the CEO walks in
on day one and asks you

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:43.600
what your current status, where
do you stand with a project,

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:46.130
you can already give a
very first hypothesis

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:48.370
without having looked
into any data at all.

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:50.240
And now, once you move to the next stage,

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:51.830
to the data analysis stage,

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:53.817
now you do not need to
look at everything, right?

00:06:53.817 --> 00:06:56.550
The only things that you need
to look at are your hypothesis

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:59.518
and try to verify or
falsify your hypothesis,

00:06:59.518 --> 00:07:00.950
because you already know

00:07:00.950 --> 00:07:03.210
if you came up with a good
and logical structure.

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:05.480
So, now let's break it down even further.

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:07.390
How would this really look like?

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:08.630
And indeed, at some point,

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:11.110
management will confront
you with an objective,

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:12.330
so you need to ask yourself, okay,

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.510
what does management
actually want to achieve?

00:07:14.510 --> 00:07:17.230
Next step is to translate
these management objectives

00:07:17.230 --> 00:07:18.800
into your project objectives.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:20.750
So, again, doesn't matter
if you're a consultant

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:22.550
or just an internal team working on it,

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:25.810
ask yourself, what does
your specific project

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:27.080
really needs to accomplish?

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:29.100
Then you're breaking down the project

00:07:29.100 --> 00:07:33.410
into its individual sub-parts,
into its key drivers.

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:35.910
Different consulting firms
give different names to this.

00:07:35.910 --> 00:07:37.990
Some like to call it modules,

00:07:37.990 --> 00:07:40.130
others like to call it workstreams,

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:41.950
others just project areas.

00:07:41.950 --> 00:07:43.820
But you often like to break it down

00:07:43.820 --> 00:07:46.000
into different sub
areas that are relevant.

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.270
And then, offer your staff,

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:50.440
every team member on
one of these key areas,

00:07:50.440 --> 00:07:52.330
or maybe one team member also addresses

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:53.900
two of these key drivers.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:55.760
But these are kind of the
key areas that you look at,

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:57.100
that you can structure the project in.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:59.120
And here, now, of course,

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:01.700
the MECE principle is super important.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:04.090
I talked about this
principle in another video.

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:05.910
I will link it also somewhere above here.

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:07.890
The MECE principle is now the principle

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:10.080
that you need to apply
when you now break down

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:12.720
the overall project
into its sub-components.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:14.660
Now, once you have defined the key drivers

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.027
that will lead you to a result,

00:08:16.027 --> 00:08:18.390
now is the time you need to
come up with a hypothesis.

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:20.590
And then, once you've
noted down your hypothesis,

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:22.620
and of course, some things
you might already know right?

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.790
But for the things that
you do not know yet,

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:26.970
ask yourself, "What are the key questions?

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:29.230
What are the analysis
that I need to do now

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:33.250
in order to falsify or
to verify my hypothesis?"

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:35.614
Because from this, you will
know exactly what analysis

00:08:35.614 --> 00:08:36.890
do you need to do.

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:39.170
This will also tell you what
data you need to collect,

00:08:39.170 --> 00:08:42.440
so this really is now your
roadmap for the project

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:45.170
in order to get to the objective
where you want to get it.

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:47.200
And in this sense now, I
hope that you recognize

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:50.440
this two-tiered structure,
this two-tiered approach

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:52.630
of this hypothesis-based problem solving.

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:54.810
So, first you structure the problem.

00:08:54.810 --> 00:08:57.660
This really crucial, and
it's an essential part

00:08:57.660 --> 00:08:59.630
of the whole problem solving process,

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:01.610
and this is also why consulting firms

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:04.910
put so much emphasis on
getting the structure right,

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:06.819
on coming up with MECE structures,

00:09:06.819 --> 00:09:09.077
because of course, if you mess up this up,

00:09:09.077 --> 00:09:12.240
then likely your whole analysis
structure will be messed up

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:14.250
and you will not get to the right results.

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:16.050
So, once you've completed your structure

00:09:16.050 --> 00:09:18.570
and you defined all
your required analysis,

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:19.510
this is, then, when you move

00:09:19.510 --> 00:09:21.140
to the second phase of your work,

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:23.700
and this is really the
whole analytical work.

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:25.510
Based on these facts that you collected,

00:09:25.510 --> 00:09:28.340
of course, you will find
some hypotheses to be true,

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:29.650
so you verified them,

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:32.270
but some other hypotheses,
now you need to reject.

00:09:32.270 --> 00:09:33.970
You will not find them to be true.

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:36.550
And in this sense, now this
is an iterative process,

00:09:36.550 --> 00:09:38.880
that whenever you falsify an hypothesis,

00:09:38.880 --> 00:09:41.190
you need to ask yourself
whether the conclusions

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:43.500
that you hypothesized now are still valid.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:45.460
So, if I take this hypothesis away,

00:09:45.460 --> 00:09:47.650
can I still defend my conclusion

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:49.780
or do I need to iterate and change it?

00:09:49.780 --> 00:09:52.720
Revise my hypothesis,
conduct some new analysis

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:54.360
and then review and update

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:57.670
my conclusions and
recommendations based on that.

00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:59.490
I hope this video was insightful.

